## Method and reasoning
Recursively split the half-open range in half, sort both halves, and merge the smallest available fronts into a buffer. Take from the left half on equality. When taking a strictly smaller right value, every remaining left value forms one inversion with it. Each recursion level uses O(n) work, and the buffer can be shared.

## Walk through the sample
In [3,1,3,2,1], the first 3 exceeds three later elements, the second 3 exceeds two, and 2 exceeds one. The equal 3s contribute zero. Choosing the left item on equality preserves this strict rule during a merge.

## Complexity
Time O(n log n), space O(n).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

long long solve(vector<int> &a, vector<int> &buffer, int l, int r) {
    if (r - l < 2)
        return 0;
    int m = l + (r - l) / 2;
    long long answer = solve(a, buffer, l, m);
    answer += solve(a, buffer, m, r);
    int i = l, j = m;
    for (int k = l; k < r; ++k) {
        if (j == r || (i < m && a[i] <= a[j]))
            buffer[k] = a[i++];
        else {
            buffer[k] = a[j++];
            answer += m - i;
        }
    }
    copy(buffer.begin() + l, buffer.begin() + r, a.begin() + l);
    return answer;
}
int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<int> a(n), buffer(n);
    for (int &x : a)
        cin >> x;
    cout << solve(a, buffer, 0, n) << '\n';
}
